<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<pretext xml:lang="en-US" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <docinfo>
    <document-id>farman-SkeletonProofs</document-id>
    <directories external="../assets"/>
    <macros>
      \newcommand{\N}{\mathbb{N}}
      \newcommand{\Z}{\mathbb{Z}}
      \newcommand{\Q}{\mathbb{Q}}
      \newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}
      \newcommand{\abs}[1]{\lvert #1 \rvert}
    </macros>
  </docinfo>

  <article xml:id="skeleton-proofs">
    <title>Skeleton Proofs</title>

    <frontmatter>
      <bibinfo>
        <author>
          <personname>Blake Farman</personname>
          <institution>Louisiana Tech University</institution>
        </author>
      </bibinfo>

      <titlepage>
        <titlepage-items/>
      </titlepage>

      <abstract>
        <p>
          Skeleton proofs for the standard proof techniques used in an
          introduction to proofs course, with worked examples.
        </p>
      </abstract>
    </frontmatter>

    <introduction xml:id="intro">
      <p>
        Adapted, with modifications, from Dana C. Ernst's <em>An
        Introduction to Proof via Inquiry-Based Learning</em>
        (<url href="https://github.com/dcernst/IBL-IntroToProof">github.com/dcernst/IBL-IntroToProof</url>),
        Skeleton Proofs 2.48, 2.49, 2.53, 2.54, 2.81<ndash/>2.85,
        &#169; Dana C. Ernst. The wording and section structure here have
        been modified from the original. Ernst's text is licensed under a
        Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License
        (<url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0</url>),
        and, per the terms of that license, so is this derivative document.
      </p>

      <p>
        The skeleton proofs below give the basic structure of the most
        common proof strategies for a conditional statement
        <m>A \implies B</m> (or a more general statement <m>P</m>),
        together with the quantified versions you will use most often.
        Filling in a skeleton is not, by itself, a proof<mdash/>the middle
        of each proof is where the actual mathematical argument goes<mdash/>
        but recognizing which skeleton you are filling in will help you
        organize your writing and will make it easier for a reader to
        follow your argument.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-direct">
      <title>Direct Proof</title>

      <p>
        Each of the proofs you have produced so far had the same format,
        which we refer to as a <term>direct proof</term>.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-direct">
        <title>Proof of <m>A \implies B</m> by Direct Proof</title>
        <p>
          If you want to prove the implication <m>A \implies B</m> via a
          direct proof, then the structure of the proof is as follows.
        </p>
        <p><em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> Assume <m>A</m>.</p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Use definitions and known results to derive
          <m>B</m>]</em> <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>Therefore, <m>B</m>.</p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-direct">
        <statement>
          <p>If <m>n</m> is an even integer, then <m>n^2</m> is even.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Assume <m>n</m> is even. By definition, there is an integer
            <m>k</m> such that <m>n = 2k</m>. Then
          </p>
          <p><md><mrow>n^2 = (2k)^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2).</mrow></md></p>
          <p>
            Since <m>2k^2 \in \Z</m>, it follows that <m>n^2</m> is even.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-contraposition">
      <title>Proof by Contraposition</title>

      <p>
        If you want to prove a conditional proposition, you can prove its
        contrapositive instead. This approach is called a
        <term>proof by contraposition</term>.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-contrapositive">
        <title>Proof of <m>A \implies B</m> by Contraposition</title>
        <p>
          If you want to prove the implication <m>A \implies B</m> by
          proving its contrapositive <m>\neg B \implies \neg A</m> instead,
          then the structure of the proof is as follows.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> We will utilize a proof
          by contraposition. Assume <m>\neg B</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Use definitions and known results to derive
          <m>\neg A</m>]</em> <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore, <m>\neg A</m>. We have proved the contrapositive, and
          hence if <m>A</m>, then <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-contrapositive">
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>n \in \Z</m>. If <m>n^2</m> is odd, then <m>n</m> is odd.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            We will utilize a proof by contraposition. Assume <m>n</m> is
            even. By definition, there is an integer <m>k</m> such that
            <m>n = 2k</m>. Then
          </p>
          <p><md><mrow>n^2 = (2k)^2 = 2(2k^2),</mrow></md></p>
          <p>
            so <m>n^2</m> is even, i.e., <m>n^2</m> is not odd. We have
            proved the contrapositive, and hence if <m>n^2</m> is odd, then
            <m>n</m> is odd.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-contradiction">
      <title>Proof by Contradiction</title>

      <p>
        Suppose that we want to prove some proposition <m>P</m> (which
        might be something like <m>A \implies B</m> or even more
        complicated). One approach, called <term>proof by
        contradiction</term>, is to assume <m>\neg P</m> and then logically
        deduce a contradiction of the form <m>Q \wedge \neg Q</m>, where
        <m>Q</m> is some proposition. Since this is absurd, the assumption
        <m>\neg P</m> must have been false, so <m>P</m> is true. The tricky
        part about a proof by contradiction is that it is not usually
        obvious what the statement <m>Q</m> should be.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-contradiction">
        <title>Proof of <m>P</m> by Contradiction</title>
        <p>
          Here is what the general structure for a proof by contradiction
          looks like if we are trying to prove the proposition <m>P</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> For sake of a
          contradiction, assume <m>\neg P</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Use definitions and known results to derive
          some <m>Q</m> and its negation <m>\neg Q</m>.]</em> <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>This is a contradiction. Therefore, <m>P</m>.</p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-contradiction-largest-even">
        <statement>
          <p>There is no largest even integer.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            For sake of a contradiction, assume that there is a largest
            even integer, say <m>m</m>. Since <m>m</m> is even, there is an
            integer <m>k</m> such that <m>m = 2k</m>, and so
          </p>
          <p><md><mrow>m + 2 = 2k + 2 = 2(k+1)</mrow></md></p>
          <p>
            is also even. Moreover, <m>m + 2 &gt; m</m>. This contradicts
            the assumption that <m>m</m> is the largest even integer.
            Therefore, there is no largest even integer.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p>
        Proof by contradiction can be useful for proving statements of the
        form <m>A \implies B</m>, where <m>\neg B</m> is easier to
        <q>get your hands on,</q> because
        <m>\neg(A \implies B)</m> is logically equivalent to
        <m>A \wedge \neg B</m>.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-conditional-contradiction">
        <title>Proof of <m>A \implies B</m> by Contradiction</title>
        <p>
          If you want to prove the implication <m>A \implies B</m> via a
          proof by contradiction, then the structure of the proof is as
          follows.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> For sake of a
          contradiction, assume <m>A</m> and <m>\neg B</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Use definitions and known results to derive
          some <m>Q</m> and its negation <m>\neg Q</m>.]</em> <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>This is a contradiction. Therefore, if <m>A</m>, then <m>B</m>.</p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-contradiction-irrational">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>x</m> be a rational number and let <m>y</m> be an
            irrational number. Then <m>x + y</m> is irrational.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            For sake of a contradiction, assume <m>x</m> is rational,
            <m>y</m> is irrational, and <m>x + y</m> is rational. Since the
            difference of two rational numbers is rational,
          </p>
          <p><md><mrow>y = (x+y) - x</mrow></md></p>
          <p>
            is rational. This contradicts the assumption that <m>y</m> is
            irrational. Therefore, if <m>x</m> is rational and <m>y</m> is
            irrational, then <m>x+y</m> is irrational.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <note xml:id="note-elegance">
        <p>
          A direct proof is usually considered more elegant than a proof by
          contradiction, and you should generally prefer one when it is
          available. A telltale sign that a proof by contradiction was
          unnecessary is when, after assuming <m>A</m> and <m>\neg B</m>,
          you derive <m>\neg B</m> and <m>B</m> themselves as your
          contradiction<mdash/>that argument can usually be rewritten as a
          direct proof of <m>A \implies B</m>. On the other hand, if the
          contradiction you reach involves some <m>Q</m> genuinely
          different from <m>B</m>, a proof by contradiction was likely the
          right tool.
        </p>
      </note>

      <example xml:id="example-unnecessary-contradiction">
        <title>An Unnecessary Proof by Contradiction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>If <m>n</m> is an even integer, then <m>n^2</m> is even.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            For sake of a contradiction, assume <m>n</m> is even and
            <m>n^2</m> is odd. By definition, there is an integer <m>k</m>
            such that <m>n = 2k</m>. Then
          </p>
          <p><md><mrow>n^2 = (2k)^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2),</mrow></md></p>
          <p>
            so <m>n^2</m> is even. This contradicts the assumption that
            <m>n^2</m> is odd. Therefore, if <m>n</m> is even, then
            <m>n^2</m> is even.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p>
        Notice that the assumption <m>\neg B</m> (that <m>n^2</m> is odd)
        was never actually used<mdash/>the computation
        <m>n^2 = 2(2k^2)</m> shows <m>n^2</m> is even using only <m>A</m>
        (that <m>n</m> is even). The contradiction we reached is exactly
        <m>B</m> and <m>\neg B</m>, which is the telltale sign from
        <xref ref="note-elegance"/> that a direct proof was available all
        along. To transform this into a direct proof, delete the
        <q>for sake of a contradiction</q> framing and the
        assumption <m>\neg B</m>, and simply conclude with the fact that
        was derived:
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Proof.</em> Assume <m>n</m> is even. By definition, there is an
        integer <m>k</m> such that <m>n = 2k</m>. Then
      </p>
      <p><md><mrow>n^2 = (2k)^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2),</mrow></md></p>
      <p>so <m>n^2</m> is even.</p>

      <p>
        This is precisely the direct proof given in
        <xref ref="example-direct"/>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-biconditionals">
      <title>Proving Biconditionals</title>

      <p>
        If we want to prove a biconditional of the form <m>A \iff B</m>, we
        need to prove both <m>A \implies B</m> and <m>B \implies A</m>. You
        should always make it clear to the reader when you are proving each
        implication. One approach is to label each subproof with
        <q>(<m>\implies</m>)</q> and
        <q>(<m>\Longleftarrow</m>)</q> (including the parentheses),
        respectively. Occasionally, you will discover that the proof of
        one implication is exactly the reverse of the proof of the other
        implication. If this happens to be the case, you may skip writing
        two subproofs and simply write a single proof that chains together
        each step using biconditionals. Such proofs will almost always be
        shorter, but can be challenging to write in an eloquent way. It is
        always a safe bet to write a separate subproof for each
        implication.
      </p>

      <p>
        When proving each implication of a biconditional, you may choose
        to utilize a direct proof, a proof by contraposition, or a proof by
        contradiction. For example, you could prove the first implication
        using a proof by contradiction and a direct proof for the second
        implication.
      </p>

      <example xml:id="example-biconditional">
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>n \in \Z</m>. Then <m>n</m> is even if and only if <m>n^2</m> is even.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            (<m>\implies</m>) This is exactly the direct proof of
            <xref ref="example-direct"/>: if <m>n</m> is even, then
            <m>n^2</m> is even.
          </p>
          <p>
            (<m>\Longleftarrow</m>) This is exactly the contrapositive
            proof of <xref ref="example-contrapositive"/>: if <m>n^2</m> is
            odd, then <m>n</m> is odd, and this is equivalent to its
            contrapositive, if <m>n^2</m> is even, then <m>n</m> is even.
          </p>
          <p>
            Since both implications hold, <m>n</m> is even if and only if
            <m>n^2</m> is even.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-quantified">
      <title>Quantified Statements</title>

      <p>
        At this point, we should be able to use our understanding of
        quantification to construct counterexamples to complicated false
        propositions and proofs of complicated true propositions. Here are
        some general proof structures for various logical forms. Assume
        <m>U</m> is the universe of discourse.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-forall-direct">
        <title>Direct Proof of <m>(\forall x)P(x)</m></title>
        <p>
          Here is the general structure for a direct proof of the
          proposition <m>(\forall x)P(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p><em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> Let <m>x \in U</m>.</p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Use definitions and known results.]</em>
          <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore, <m>P(x)</m> is true. Since <m>x</m> was arbitrary, for
          all <m>x</m>, <m>P(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-forall-direct">
        <statement>
          <p>For every integer <m>n</m>, <m>n^2 - n</m> is even.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Let <m>n \in \Z</m>. Note that <m>n^2 - n = n(n-1)</m> is a
            product of two consecutive integers, so one of <m>n</m> and
            <m>n - 1</m> is even; write that even factor as <m>2k</m> for
            some <m>k \in \Z</m>. Then <m>n(n-1) = 2k \cdot (\text{the
            other factor})</m>, which is twice an integer. Therefore,
            <m>n^2 - n</m> is even. Since <m>n</m> was arbitrary, for all
            <m>n \in \Z</m>, <m>n^2 - n</m> is even.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p>
        Combining <xref ref="assemblage-forall-direct" text="title"/> with
        <xref ref="assemblage-direct" text="title"/>, we obtain the following skeleton
        proof.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-forall-conditional">
        <title>Proof of <m>(\forall x)(A(x) \implies B(x))</m></title>
        <p>
          Below is the general structure for a direct proof of the
          proposition <m>(\forall x)(A(x) \implies B(x))</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> Let <m>x \in U</m>.
          Assume <m>A(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Use definitions and known results to derive
          <m>B(x)</m>]</em> <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>Therefore, <m>B(x)</m>.</p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-forall-conditional">
        <statement>
          <p>For every integer <m>n</m>, if <m>6</m> divides <m>n</m>, then <m>3</m> divides <m>n</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Let <m>n \in \Z</m>. Assume <m>6</m> divides <m>n</m>. By
            definition, there is an integer <m>k</m> such that
            <m>n = 6k</m>. Then
          </p>
          <p><md><mrow>n = 6k = 3(2k),</mrow></md></p>
          <p>
            and since <m>2k \in \Z</m>, it follows that <m>3</m> divides
            <m>n</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-forall-contradiction">
        <title>Proof of <m>(\forall x)P(x)</m> by Contradiction</title>
        <p>
          Here is the general structure for a proof of the proposition
          <m>(\forall x)P(x)</m> via contradiction.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> For sake of a
          contradiction, assume that there exists <m>x \in U</m> such that
          <m>\neg P(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Do something to derive a contradiction.]</em>
          <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a contradiction. Therefore, for all <m>x</m>, <m>P(x)</m>
          is true.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-forall-contradiction">
        <statement>
          <p>For every integer <m>n</m>, <m>n^2 \neq 2</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            For sake of a contradiction, assume that there exists
            <m>n \in \Z</m> such that <m>n^2 = 2</m>. Since
            <m>1^2 = 1 &lt; 2 &lt; 4 = 2^2</m>, we have
            <m>1 &lt; \abs{n} &lt; 2</m>. But there is no integer strictly
            between <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>. This is a contradiction.
            Therefore, for all <m>n \in \Z</m>, <m>n^2 \neq 2</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-exists-direct">
        <title>Direct Proof of <m>(\exists x)P(x)</m></title>
        <p>
          Here is the general structure for a direct proof of the
          proposition <m>(\exists x)P(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p><em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> <m>\ldots</m></p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Use definitions, axioms, and previous results
          to deduce that an <m>x</m> exists for which <m>P(x)</m> is true;
          or if you have an <m>x</m> that works, just verify that it
          does.]</em> <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>Therefore, there exists <m>x \in U</m> such that <m>P(x)</m>.</p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-exists-direct">
        <statement>
          <p>There exists an even prime number.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The number <m>2</m> is prime, and <m>2 = 2 \cdot 1</m> is even.
            Therefore, there exists an even prime number.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-exists-contradiction">
        <title>Proof of <m>(\exists x)P(x)</m> by Contradiction</title>
        <p>
          Below is the general structure for a proof of the proposition
          <m>(\exists x)P(x)</m> via contradiction.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>[State any upfront assumptions.]</em> For sake of a
          contradiction, assume that for all <m>x \in U</m>,
          <m>\neg P(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\ldots</m> <em>[Do something to derive a contradiction.]</em>
          <m>\ldots</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a contradiction. Therefore, there exists <m>x \in U</m>
          such that <m>P(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <example xml:id="example-exists-contradiction">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Among any three consecutive integers <m>n, n+1, n+2</m>, at
            least one is divisible by <m>3</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            For sake of a contradiction, assume that none of <m>n</m>,
            <m>n+1</m>, <m>n+2</m> is divisible by <m>3</m>. Every integer
            leaves a remainder of <m>0</m>, <m>1</m>, or <m>2</m> upon
            division by <m>3</m>; since <m>n</m> is not divisible by
            <m>3</m>, either <m>n</m> leaves remainder <m>1</m> or
            remainder <m>2</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>If <m>n</m> leaves remainder <m>1</m>, then <m>n + 2</m>
              leaves remainder <m>0</m>, so <m>3</m> divides <m>n+2</m>.</li>
              <li>If <m>n</m> leaves remainder <m>2</m>, then <m>n + 1</m>
              leaves remainder <m>0</m>, so <m>3</m> divides <m>n+1</m>.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
          <p>
            Either way, one of <m>n</m>, <m>n+1</m>, <m>n+2</m> is
            divisible by <m>3</m>, contradicting our assumption.
            Therefore, at least one of <m>n, n+1, n+2</m> is divisible by
            <m>3</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <note xml:id="note-counterexample">
        <p>
          A counterexample to a false statement <m>(\forall x)Q(x)</m> is
          exactly a proof of <m>(\exists x)(\neg Q(x))</m>, so it can be
          produced by following <xref ref="assemblage-exists-direct" text="title"/>.
          Also, several of these skeleton proofs are sometimes combined in
          a single proof: for instance, to prove
          <m>(\forall x)(P(x) \implies Q(x))</m> by contradiction, you
          would begin by assuming that there exists <m>x \in U</m> such
          that <m>P(x)</m> and <m>\neg Q(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </note>
    </section>

  </article>

</pretext>
